 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nBearerToken.create_token_response breaks with callable expires_in\n\n#### Description\nWhen using `BearerToken` with a callable `expires_in` parameter, token generation fails. The code doesn't properly handle callable `expires_in` values anymore.\n\n#### Steps to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth2.rfc6749.tokens import BearerToken\nfrom oauthlib.common import Request\n\n# Create a token generator with callable expires_in\ndef dynamic_expires_in(request):\n    return 3600  # 1 hour\n\n# Create a token generator\ntoken_generator = BearerToken(\n    token_generator=lambda request: 'access_token_value',\n    expires_in=dynamic_expires_in,\n    refresh_token_generator=lambda request, refresh_token=False: 'refresh_token_value'\n)\n\n# Create a request\nrequest = Request('https://example.com/token')\nrequest.scopes = ['read', 'write']\n\n# This fails\ntoken = token_generator.create_token_response(request, refresh_token=True)\n```\n\n#### Expected Results\nThe token generator should properly handle callable `expires_in` values and include the correct expiration time in the token response.\n\n#### Actual Results\nThe token generation fails because the code tries to use `self.expires_in` directly without checking if it's callable. This causes issues when trying to include the expiration time in the token response.\n\nWhen using a callable `expires_in`, the token generation process breaks because the code doesn't evaluate the callable to get the actual expiration time.\n</issue>\n",
